  "content": {
    "title": "Trump admin proposing 'catastrophic' cuts to the National Park Service",
    "text": "FILE: Grand Canyon National Park ranger Jill Staurowsky looks out from the South Rim while giving a tour to visitors on Feb. 22, 2025, in Grand Canyon, Ariz.Brandon Bell/Getty ImagesThe National Park Service will operate with more than 25% less money if the Trump administration gets its wish. According to the administration’s 2027 budget proposal, released Friday, funding for park operations could be reduced by $736 million. “A cut this massive would be catastrophic,” said John Garder, senior director of budget and appropriations for the National Parks Conservation Association, in a news release. “After a year of deep staffing cuts, dwindling resources, and attacks on history and science, park staff are already at the brink. Park maintenance needs are growing, protections are eroding, and visitor experience is declining. This proposal would only accelerate the damage, putting our national parks at even greater risk and further cutting the park staff needed to care for our national treasures.”Article continues below this adAt the same time, the Trump administration is proposing the allocation of $10 billion within the Park Service for a new “Presidential Capital Stewardship Program” for beautification efforts around Washington, D.C., leading up to the country’s 250th anniversary this summer. That’s more than three times the annual budget of the Park Service, and nearly half of the Park Service’s total deferred maintenance backlog across the entire system. “We support efforts to modernize and repair park infrastructure but not when it’s paired with massive cuts to Park Service operations,” Garder said. The Trump administration’s proposed budget, which would need to be approved by Congress, also calls for cutting the Park Service’s construction budget by 72% compared to 2025. FILE: A National Park Service worker talks to visitors at the Mariposa Grove of Giant Sequoias in Yosemite National Park.Jim West/UCG/Universal Images Group via Getty ImagesThe administration is looking to cull staff, too: A new “strategic initiative” announced by the Interior Department on Thursday seeks to further slash the size of the already-reduced Park Service. Article continues below this adMake SFGATE a preferred source so your search results prioritize writing by actual people, not AI.Add Preferred SourceA statement from the department said it would be improving “resource management and the delivery of critical services nationwide” by modernizing the department, which includes the National Park Service, the Bureau of Land Management, the Fish and Wildlife Service, and other entities. “By evaluating core functions and improving coordination across Interior’s 13 bureaus and offices that are charged with managing the vast natural and cultural resources of the nation, the Department will accelerate decision‑making and enhance accountability,” the news release stated.Interior employees will be offered deferred resignations or voluntary early retirement as part of the initiative. Bill Wade, executive director of the Association of National Park Rangers, called the move a “transparent attempt to push out our most experienced stewards in the name of efficiency,” in a news release. Employees must be at least 50 years old and have 20 years of federal service, or be any age with 25 years of service, to qualify for the early retirement option.The Trump administration has already cut or lost almost 25% of the Interior’s workforce through similar programs and “reductions-in-force” throughout last year. That includes mass terminations that began on Feb. 14, 2025, severance payments offered in March 2025, and more. The Park Service was short thousands of seasonal employees last summer. Article continues below this ad“Apparently Elon Musk’s destruction wasn’t enough,” said Center for Western Priorities Deputy Director Aaron Weiss in a news release. “Now [Interior Secretary] Doug Burgum is planning another round of cuts across a department that’s already stretched to the breaking point.”A park ranger smiles from a booth at the entrance to Zion National Park in Springdale, Utah, on March 7, 2026.Michael Yanow/NurPhoto via Getty ImagesNational Park Service staff will be shifted from their existing positions to “visitor-facing roles,” the news release said. The Department of Interior did not immediately respond to what kinds of jobs this would entail, or what positions would become unstaffed as part of the change.“The department’s claim that it will align more positions to visitor-facing roles is an ominous signal, suggesting that the administration is preparing to abandon the vital, behind the scenes work that keeps our national parks alive,” Wade said in the news release. Article continues below this adGot a tip? Send us the scoop.Got a tip? Send us the scoop.DO IT NOW The initiative’s stated goals include strengthening “Interior’s mission of stewardship and service, optimizing operations, streamlining outdated bureaucracy and ensuring meaningful economic benefits for taxpayers and communities across the country.” But it’s unclear how fewer staff, and a significantly reduced budget, will lead to such outcomes. Parks are already struggling to keep up with basic tasks: Yosemite National Park’s entrances have been wide open this spring, with repeated instances of no ranger staffing gates to collect fees. Yosemite employees who spoke with SFGATE under the condition of anonymity to protect their jobs, in accordance with Hearst’s ethics policy, said there aren’t enough employees to cover all the usual shifts.“The administration’s most recent reorganization plans won’t solve the challenges our parks are facing,” said Emily Douce, acting vice president of the National Parks Conservation Association, in a statement to SFGATE.Douce said the changes are antithetical to the purpose of the Park Service. “This latest attempt to push park staff out and prioritize visitor-facing roles at all costs runs counter to the very mission the Park Service was created to uphold and the natural and cultural resources it is mandated to protect,” she said. “You can only maintain the appearance of things being fine for so long. The cracks are already beginning to show, and this approach will only widen them.”Article continues below this adWe love national parks just as much as you do, so we have a newsletter that covers them from top to bottom. Sign up here.April 3, 2026Big Sky Country Contributing Parks EditorKylie Mohr is the Big Sky Country contributing parks editor at SFGATE, covering Glacier, Yellowstone and Grand Teton national parks from her home in Montana. She’s an award-winning freelance journalist and correspondent for the magazine High Country News, where her work focuses on wildfire, wildlife and wild places in the West. Her bylines include the Atlantic, National Geographic, Outside, Vox, Business Insider, Grist and more. She’s traipsed through the tundra banding snowy owls, climbed to the top of a 300-foot-tall tree and become a wildland firefighter for her stories. Send story tips or comments to kylie.mohr@sfgate.com.",
